I just checked Fire and Mud. The Pinatubo RSAM was still the Model 100 vers=
ion.  From the USGS openb-file report on the Model 100 version

"At the beginning of each minute, a call to the data-acquisition sub-progra=
m causes the Model 100/102 to digitize 125 samples for each seismic input a=
t a rate of about 50 samples/second/input and return the sums of the digiti=
zed values."

So the event data would have been the RSAM average taken over about 2.5 sec=
onds (125 samples/(50 samples/second)). I think you understand that everyth=
ing is approximate because of the limitations of the Model 100. You couldn'=
t sample the data in background like I was able to when I converted to the =
PC-based RSAM.  I recall that I got to 50 samples/second/input by just expe=
riementing with a simple loop interation delay between taking a sample unti=
l it seemed to be close to the desired 50 sps /input channel.

So the Model 100 would grab 125 samples, halt digitizing for a bit to proce=
ss the data and then go back for another 125 samples. The RSAM openfile est=
imates that over a minute less than 15% of the time (~9 seconds/minute) was=
 devoted to processing and transferring the data for BOB storage. Not sampl=
ing during that 9 seconds was another reason for the classical earthquake s=
eismologists to initially dismiss RSAM.

When I went to the PC-based RSAM I think I went to 100 samples/second, but =
would have to double-check that.


